<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mạc Đăng Dung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1483–1541</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Mac Dang Dung</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mạc Đăng Dung was a Vietnamese general who usurped the throne of the Later Lê dynasty in 1527, founding the Mạc dynasty. A fisherman's son who rose to command the palace guard, he exploited the weakness of the Lê court. Lê loyalists rallied in the south, beginning a long civil war. In 1540 he submitted to the Ming to avert an invasion, accepting a lesser title.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mac-dang-dung.jpg" alt="Mạc Đăng Dung" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Feng Shi Yang (馮時暘), Liang Tian Xi (梁天錫), Jiang Mei Zhong (江美中) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_Nan_Lai_Wei_Tu_Ce.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
